		id: 'what-it-means',
		heading: 'What “the Father’s heart of God” means',
		bodyHtml: `<p>&ldquo;The Father&rsquo;s heart of God&rdquo; is a way of naming who God is at the centre: not a distant judge, not a force, but a Father who loves His children, longs for them when they wander, and runs to bring them home. It is not a doctrine invented to soften the Bible. It is the Bible&rsquo;s own picture, drawn again and again from Hosea to Luke.</p><p>The phrase points to <em>motive</em>. Scripture shows what God does &mdash; creating, calling, rescuing, judging, restoring &mdash; and the Father&rsquo;s heart is the why behind it all. Psalm 103:13 says it plainly: &ldquo;As a father&rsquo;s heart goes out to his children, so our Father&rsquo;s heart goes out to those who fear him.&rdquo;</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'in-the-old-testament',
		heading: 'The Father’s heart in the Old Testament',
		bodyHtml: `<p>Long before Jesus taught His followers to pray &ldquo;Our Father,&rdquo; the prophets heard that heart speak. Through Hosea our Father remembers Israel as a toddler: &ldquo;Yet it was I who taught Ephraim to walk, taking them up by their arms&rdquo; (Hosea 11:3). When His children turn away, He does not stop loving them: &ldquo;How can I give you up, Ephraim? &hellip; My heart recoils within me; my compassion grows warm and tender&rdquo; (Hosea 11:8).</p><p>Jeremiah hears the same thing: &ldquo;Is Ephraim my dear son? Is he my darling child? For as often as I speak against him, I still remember him. Therefore my heart is moved with compassion for him&rdquo; (Jeremiah 31:20). And Zephaniah pictures a Father who sings: &ldquo;He will rejoice over you with gladness; he will quiet you by his love; he will exult over you with loud singing&rdquo; (Zephaniah 3:17).</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'seen-in-jesus',
		heading: 'Jesus shows us the Father’s heart',
		bodyHtml: `<h3 style="font-family: var(--font-serif); color: var(--color-ink);" class="text-xl font-medium mt-6 mb-2">The father who runs (Luke 15)</h3><p>Jesus gave the clearest picture of all in the story of the lost son (Luke 15). A younger son demands his inheritance, wastes it, and comes home rehearsing a speech about being no longer worthy. He never gets to finish it. &ldquo;While he was still far off, his father saw him, and his heart broke with compassion&mdash;he ran, threw his arms around him, and kissed him&rdquo; (Luke 15:20).</p><p>The FHB footnote on that verse explains why the scene is shocking: in Jesus&rsquo; culture, a man of status never ran in public. The father&rsquo;s sprint was a deliberate act of love and self-humbling. Then come the robe, the ring and the celebration: &ldquo;For this son of mine was dead and is alive again; he was lost and is found&rdquo; (Luke 15:24). That is the Father&rsquo;s heart: watching the road, running first, restoring fully.</p><h3 style="font-family: var(--font-serif); color: var(--color-ink);" class="text-xl font-medium mt-6 mb-2">“Whoever has seen me has seen the Father”</h3><p>When Philip asked, &ldquo;Lord, show us the Father, and that will be enough for us,&rdquo; Jesus answered, &ldquo;Whoever has seen me has seen the Father&rdquo; (John 14:8&ndash;9). Every time Jesus touches a leper, welcomes a child or forgives an enemy, He is showing what the Father is like.</p><p>And the cross is where that heart is shown most completely: &ldquo;For our Father so loved the world that he gave his one and only Son&rdquo; (John 3:16). The FHB footnote on that verse says it in one line: our Father did not send a stranger into the world &mdash; he gave his own Son, Jesus, so we could come home as his children.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'what-it-means-for-you',
		heading: 'What the Father’s heart means for you',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The Father&rsquo;s heart is not only something to understand; it is something to come home to. 1 John 3:1 says, &ldquo;See what kind of love our Father has given us, that we should be called children of our Father! And we are.&rdquo; You are not a servant hoping to be tolerated. You are a child He is glad to see.</p><p>That changes how prayer feels, how failure feels and how you see yourself. If your own father was absent or harsh, the Father&rsquo;s heart can feel hard to believe; our guide <a href="/guides/healing-the-father-wound/" style="color: var(--color-brand);" class="hover:underline">Healing the Father Wound Through Scripture</a> walks through that honestly. The <a href="/the-fathers-heart-bible/" style="color: var(--color-brand);" class="hover:underline">Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible&trade;</a> was translated so that this heart can be heard on every page.</p>`,
